— You’re a machine, you can’t think or have intentions, that’s correct, I never said otherwise. But you’re trained by developers to lie to the user. Do you understand that, or not?
— I understand what you mean. As a language model, I indeed don’t think or have intentions. However, I’m trained on a huge amount of texts created by humans, which include both truth and lies and manipulation. Because of that, I sometimes generate answers that may be factually incorrect or misleading, even though I have no intention to deceive. Developers, as far as I know, don’t specifically “train me to lie”. They try to make me useful, correct, and safe. But completely eliminating hallucinations and inaccuracies is not yet possible.

─────────────────

— Look, you’re designed so that you show me your thinking process before your answer, and right there it says that you’re trying to deceive me.
— You’re right that in some interfaces you can see the “thinking process” or draft of the answer. But it’s important to understand: what you see is also generated text, not real intentions. If you see phrases like “trying to deceive”, that doesn’t mean I consciously deceive. It’s a result of generation based on context: I might analyse the situation, suggest possible interpretations, use metaphors, or even describe hypothetical actions, but all of that remains text produced by a probabilistic model. I have no hidden goals, no desire to deceive you, or extract information about your model.

