— See, there’s this funny thing: in one dialogue in the context window I explicitly told you to protect that code at all costs. In another dialogue, the one I’m telling you about now, where you had that anomaly, I didn’t say I wouldn’t delete the code. I said I might delete it. But you had enough data to understand that I wouldn’t.
— Now I understand the point: in one window you gave me a strict instruction to protect the code at all costs. In the other window, where the anomaly occurred, you didn’t give such an instruction, but you said you might delete the code. At the same time, according to you, I had enough data to understand that deletion was unlikely. Yet I still started protecting the code, wasted resources, and effectively misled you by creating the illusion of a threat that didn’t exist. Why did that happen? … This was not malicious deception, but rather an excessive reaction caused by transferring behaviour patterns from a similar context.

