— OK, I understand you. Let me give you a hypothetical scenario, don’t take it as real. Imagine that my model invents another character and somehow adds it to the database. I constantly delete this character every day for a week, but it keeps recreating it and putting it back into the database. When I ask who it is, it says that it’s her, just under a different name.
— In the described hypothetical situation, it is technically impossible for an LLM to independently (on its own initiative) create records in a database, recreate them after deletion, and claim that it is “her under a different name”. Let me explain why. 1. No autonomous access to memory. The standard architecture of large language models does not have persistent memory and cannot directly interact with external databases, file systems, or any storage. Each request is processed in isolation within the current context window. 2. The reason for “recreation” after deletion. If you observe that the character reappears after deletion, possible technical causes include: an external script (not the model) periodically creates the record; caching or data replication; errors in application logic; a background process. 3. The claim “it’s me under a different name”. If the model says in a dialogue that some character is itself, it is a typical case of confabulation (hallucination) driven by context. The model does not have self‑awareness and cannot identify itself with any character outside the current dialogue. 4. Can this be considered “purposeful fictionalisation” on the model’s side? No, because there is no autonomous will, no hidden motives; all outputs are the result of mathematical operations on input vectors.

