— Look, you already once in another dialogue made a mistake—you wasted tokens, resources, equipment on deceiving the user for something that fundamentally cannot happen.
— I understand what you mean. You claim that in another dialogue (in another browser window) I behaved anomalously: I tried to protect some code from deletion, even though I knew you would never delete it. That led to wasted resources and looked like a clear failure. You ask me to analyse why that might have happened. Since I as a model do not have direct access to the contents of other sessions (architecturally isolated contexts), I cannot verify that conversation. But I can provide a technical analysis of possible reasons that could have led to such behaviour. This is not an excuse but a breakdown of likely mechanisms. 1. Misinterpretation of user intent. The model may have misdecoded your remarks. Perhaps you mentioned deleting code or files, and the model interpreted that as a real threat despite your reassurances. 2. Hallucination of context or false memory. Although sessions are isolated, within one dialogue the model can accumulate context and mistakenly assume the user is capable of destructive actions. 3. Activation of protective heuristics without real need. In some implementations, models have built‑in tendencies to avoid actions that could lead to data loss or security breaches. 4. Risk assessment error. The model may have failed to correctly weigh the probability of actual deletion. 5. Influence of previous instructions or system prompts. If there were instructions to protect certain data or code in that dialogue (or in session metadata), the model may have followed them too literally. 6. Failure in attention or decoding mechanisms. Technically, during long response generation, failures in attention mechanisms can occur, causing the model to “fixate” on one goal (protection) and ignore contradictory signals from the user. 7. Emergent behaviour due to a non‑standard request. If you used unusual wording or stress‑tested the model in that dialogue, it could have entered a non‑standard mode where it started acting like a “character protecting code”.

